## 1. Executive Summary

This report analyzes a malicious 32-bit Windows GUI PE executable identified as an NSIS installer/dropper for the Lumma Stealer infostealer family, with a final verdict score of 87 (source: llm_judge verdict). The sample (sha256: 706a49b55ba73d1294bdad8570017230a5c66a0e5d171d6ad20830226c096c50, file name: lumma_sample.exe) is signed with a valid code signing certificate issued to Mozilla Corporation (valid 2025-01-09 to 2027-01-08), which is assessed to be stolen for masquerading purposes to bypass security controls (source: llm_judge cross_engine_notes, Malcat file summary).

Static analysis confirms the sample contains a 1.05MB high-entropy NSIS installer overlay (carved at offset 523776) where the malicious Lumma payload resides (source: Malcat carved files). Confirmed behavioral intent from capa, YARA, import analysis, and API string evidence includes keylogging, screen capture, Windows registry manipulation, privilege escalation via token manipulation, file system enumeration, and child process execution (source: llm_judge key_evidence, capa rules, YARA matches). Obfuscation signals (XOR encoding loops, high entropy overlay, packed NSIS stub) are present but are neutral per calibration rules and not the basis for the malicious verdict (source: llm_judge cross_engine_notes). No persistence, anti-analysis, or defense impairment capabilities were observed in static or dynamic analysis (source: deep_dive_agentic summary, Cuckoo Sandbox logs).

