## 3. File Layout & Structural Analysis

The sample is a packed NSIS installer with a large overlay containing the malicious payload. The Malcat file layout table below details the PE sections and overlay:

| Name | EA | Physical Size | Virtual Size | Entropy | Rights |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| header | 0 | 1024 | 0 | 1.24 | - |
| .text | 1024 | 28672 | 28672 | 1.43 | RX |
| .rdata | 29696 | 11264 | 12288 | 0.84 | R |
| .data | 41984 | 512 | 425984 | 0 | RW |
| .rsrc | 467968 | 4608 | 28672 | 1.76 | R |
| .reloc | 496640 | 4096 | 4096 | 0 | R |
| overlay | 500736 | 1092157 | 0 | 2.22 | - |
| .ndata | 1592893 | 0 | 675840 | 0 | RW |

*Source: Malcat file layout*

The 1.05MB overlay at EA 0x7A100 (physical offset 523776) is the NSIS installer payload bundle, confirmed by the carved NSIS file entry and YARA `Nullsoft_PiMP_Stub_SFX` and `nsis_overlay_data` matches (source: Malcat carved files, YARA matches). The .data section has a massive mismatch between physical (512 bytes) and virtual (425,984 bytes) size, a common anomaly in packed/installer binaries (source: Malcat anomalies `UnbalancedVirtualPhysicalRatio`).

Malcat identified 12 structural anomalies, including 4 instances of XOR-in-loop obfuscation in the NSIS stub, a missing PE checksum, a gap in the resource directory, and high-entropy unmarked buffers:

| Anomaly Name | Level | Category | Hits | Locations (EA) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| RelocSectionNoRelocation | 4 | sections | 1 | - |
| ResourceDirectoryGap | 4 | resources | 1 | 479602 |
| BigBufferNoXrefMediumToHighEntropy | 3 | entropy | 1 | - |
| ManyHighValueImmediates | 3 | code | 1 | 22305 |
| ManyUniqueImmediateBytes | 3 | code | 1 | 2464 |
| StackArrayInitialisationX86 | 3 | code | 1 | - |
| XorInLoop | 3 | code | 4 | 1497, 13355, 26614, 26670 |
| HighEntropy | 2 | entropy | 0 | - |
| InvalidSizeOfInitializedData | 2 | sections | 1 | - |
| InvalidSizeOfUninitializedData | 2 | sections | 1 | - |
| NoChecksum | 1 | integrity | 1 | 296 |
| UnbalancedVirtualPhysicalRatio | 1 | sections | 1 | - |

*Source: Malcat anomalies*

The 4 `XorInLoop` anomalies are consistent with NSIS stub deobfuscation logic used to unpack the embedded payload (source: Malcat anomalies). The `NoChecksum` anomaly at EA 0x128 (296) indicates the PE header checksum is unset, common in packed binaries (source: Malcat anomalies).

Carved files from the overlay include a 1.05MB NSIS installer payload, a PKCS7 code signature blob, and image resources:

| Carved File Name | Type | Size |
|---|---|---|
| ? | PNG | 11138 |
| ? | DIB | 9832 |
| ? | DIB | 4392 |
| ? | NSIS | 1055469 |
| ? | PKCS7 | 13639 |

*Source: Malcat carved files*

A discrepancy exists between reverse engineering tool counts: Ghidra reports 0 functions and 0 imports, while IDA reports 97 functions and 172 imports, and Malcat reports 15 top functions and 172 imports (source: audit trail ghidra_query, ida_query). This gap is caused by Ghidra failing to analyze the large high-entropy NSIS overlay; the empty counts are a data analysis limitation, not an indicator of benign content (source: llm_judge cross_engine_notes). UPX unpacking was attempted but failed, as the sample uses NSIS packing rather than UPX compression (source: UPX unpack output: upx_ok: False, is_packed: False).

